\\(-3\\). Now we need to determine how many such pairs there are. The sequence alternates between increasing by 3 and decreasing by 3, so the pairs will be of the form: \\((1 - 4), (7 - 10), (13 - 16), \\ldots, (49 - 52)\\).\n\nThe sequence of numbers is: 1, 7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37, 43, 49, 55, ...\n\nWe need to find how many such pairs there are. The sequence of numbers can be written as \\(1, 1+4k, 1+12k, \\ldots, 49\\), where \\(k\\) is a non-negative integer. The 17th term in this sequence is 49. Therefore, there are 8 pairs plus the 55 at the end, so there are 9 pairs in total. Since each pair sums to \\(-3\\), the total sum of all the pairs is:\n\n\\[ 9 \\times (-3) = -27 \\]\n\nFinally, we add the 55 at the end:\n\n\\[ -27 + 55 = 28 \\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{28}\\)",
